 - **Regulator:** Privacy Protection Authority (הרשות להגנת הפרטיות (HaRashut LeHaganat HaPratiyut)). A unit within the Ministry of Justice, headed by the Registrar of Databases, rather than a separate independent statutory body. Since Amendment 13 it supervises, inspects, investigates (including criminal investigation of privacy offences) and imposes financial sanctions.
 - **Law:** Protection of Privacy Law, 5741-1981, with Amendment 13 (published 14 August 2024, in force 14 August 2025) and the Protection of Privacy (Data Security) Regulations 2017
-- **Who penalises:** Regulator: since 14 August 2025 the PPA imposes financial sanctions itself, without going to court. Under Amendment 13 sanctions are calculated per violation from base amounts that vary by provision (secondary sources cite ILS 150,000 for key breaches and ILS 320,000 for some security breaches), scaled by the number of data subjects in the database and doubled for very large databases or repeat violations, with an overall ceiling reported as 5% of annual turnover. Secondary sources differ on the figures and the statute was not checked. Before Amendment 13 the PPA could only impose small fines (around ILS 10,000 to 25,000).
-- **Public bodies:** Public bodies are within the regime and must appoint data protection officers; secondary sources indicate they can receive financial sanctions, but this was not confirmed from the statute.
+- **Who penalises:** Regulator: since 14 August 2025 the PPA imposes financial sanctions itself, without going to court. Under Amendment 13 sanctions are calculated per violation from base amounts that vary by provision (secondary sources cite ILS 150,000 for key breaches and ILS 320,000 for some security breaches), scaled by the number of data subjects in the database and doubled for very large databases or repeat violations, with an overall ceiling reported as 5% of annual turnover. Secondary sources differ on the figures and the statute was not checked, but the PPA's published decisions show the method at work: a base of ILS 640,000 for late breach reporting on a high-security database of more than a million people (Menora, October 2026), ILS 40,000 for each of two documentation breaches at a municipality (Beit Shemesh) and ILS 20,000 for an incomplete collection notice (Karting), each then reduced for mitigating factors such as a clean record. Before Amendment 13 the PPA could only impose small fines (around ILS 10,000 to 25,000).
+- **Public bodies:** Public bodies are within the regime and must appoint data protection officers. Local authorities can be sanctioned: the PPA imposed ILS 64,000 on the Beit Shemesh Municipality in September 2026. No sanction on a government ministry has been published; the Ministry of Education finding of October 2026 carried none, but its supervision ran under the powers in force before Amendment 13.
 - **Appeal:** Magistrates' Court, according to a 2026 practice guide (not confirmed from the statute). Deadline: 45 days, according to a 2026 practice guide (not confirmed from the statute). Payment: No, unless the head of the PPA agrees or the court orders a stay, according to a 2026 practice guide (not confirmed from the statute).
 - **Suing directly:** Yes. Infringement of privacy is a civil wrong under the Protection of Privacy Law (s 4), actionable in the Magistrates' or District Courts depending on value, and individuals can sue without going to the PPA. Statutory compensation without proof of harm: for general privacy infringements under s 29A, reported as up to about ILS 50,000 (index-linked, around ILS 65,000) and doubled where the infringement was intended to harm, giving the ILS 100,000 figure cited by some sources; Amendment 13 added statutory damages of up to ILS 10,000 for certain database breaches such as failure to notify or correct data. Exact current amounts not confirmed from the statute.
-- **Case: Sanction on a National Insurance Institute employee (2025).** A law firm reported that in August 2025 the PPA imposed ILS 75,000 on a National Insurance Institute employee for 15 instances of accessing personal data in the Institute's systems for non-professional purposes, in breach of s 8(b). Whether this was imposed under the new Amendment 13 regime is not confirmed; the PPA decision was not checked.
-- **Case: Meuhedet Health Fund: first reported Amendment 13 sanction (2026).** Reported as the first financial sanction under Amendment 13: on 21 July 2026 the PPA imposed ILS 256,000 on the Meuhedet Health Fund for failing to report a serious security event immediately under the Data Security Regulations, after it learned of unauthorised access to medical records in November 2025 but reported only in late January 2026. The amount reflects statutory reductions. From a consultancy summary, not the PPA decision.
+- **Case: Administrative fine on a former National Insurance employee (2025).** On 16 September 2025 the PPA published an administrative fine of ILS 75,000, set in August 2025, on a former National Insurance Institute employee who had run 20 queries in the Institute's databases in 2020 and 2021, 15 of them on the sensitive data of a complainant and her family, for personal reasons, in breach of s 8(b). This is the older administrative fine, not an Amendment 13 monetary sanction: the conduct predates the amendment.
+- **Case: Meuhedet Health Fund: first Amendment 13 sanction (2026).** On 21 July 2026 the PPA imposed ILS 256,000 on the Meuhedet Health Fund, its first monetary sanction since Amendment 13 came into force, for not reporting a serious security event immediately (reg. 11(d) of the Data Security Regulations). A member told the fund in November 2025 that he could see a relative's medical file; the fault proved systemic and was reported only on 27 January 2026, about two months after the complaint. The PPA held that a preliminary report is due as soon as the event is known. The amount is after statutory reductions, which the notice does not itemise.
+- **Case: Beit Shemesh Municipality: a local authority sanctioned (2026).** On 16 September 2026 the PPA imposed ILS 64,000 on the Beit Shemesh Municipality after a fault in its GIS system exposed welfare and medical data on about 4,600 residents. Neither breach was the fault itself: a vendor with access to the database for almost two years was not listed as a holder (reg. 2(a)(7)), and the security procedure did not govern vendors' access (reg. 15(a)(3)). The PPA rejected the argument that Amendment 13 had come into force only a week before the event, since there had been a year to prepare. The base of ILS 80,000 (40,000 per breach) was reduced by 20% for no sanctions in the previous five years.
+- **Case: Menora Mivtachim Insurance: the reduction calculation in full (2026).** On 22 October 2026 the PPA imposed ILS 256,000 on Menora Mivtachim Insurance for reporting more than two weeks late that an insurance agent had been able to see a report on 138 policyholders of another agent (reg. 11(d)(1)). The notice publishes the calculation: a base of ILS 640,000, because the database is at high security level and holds data on more than a million people, reduced by a cumulative 60% under the Fifth Schedule to the Law (20% for no violations in five years, 30% for ending the breach on its own initiative and reporting it, 10% for having appointed a DPO). The duty to report does not wait for an internal investigation to finish.
 - The regulator sits inside the Ministry of Justice rather than being a separately constituted independent authority, yet Israel has held an EU adequacy decision since 2011.
-- Until August 2025 the regulator had almost no fining power; Amendment 13 gave it GDPR-style administrative sanctions, though the first reported sanction under the new regime came almost a year later.
+- Until August 2025 the regulator had almost no fining power. Amendment 13 gave it GDPR-style administrative sanctions, but the first came almost a year later: by the end of October 2026 the PPA had published four, Meuhedet (ILS 256,000, 21 July 2026), A.D. Karting Hutzot (ILS 12,000, 5 August 2026, for a registration form that took ID numbers and a child's details without the notice s 11 requires), the Beit Shemesh Municipality (ILS 64,000, 16 September 2026) and Menora Mivtachim Insurance (ILS 256,000, 22 October 2026). Two of the four are for late breach reporting.
+- The PPA also publishes findings of violation that carry no sanction, usually because the events predate Amendment 13: the Ministry of Education (8 October 2026, an open link that exposed data on about 21,000 special-education pupils), the Technion (26 October 2026, an intrusion not reported immediately) and four earlier findings in its 2026 administrative enforcement register.
 - Individuals can claim statutory compensation without proving any harm, which makes class actions attractive.
 - The same authority runs both administrative inspections and criminal investigations of privacy offences.
 - Sanctions are scaled by the number of people in the database, not only by the seriousness of the conduct.
